From: Kenneth Kasilag <kenneth@kasilag.me>
Date: Sun, 05 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: offload PPPoE flows

Offload flows of a PPPoE WAN. Consume the channel lifecycle events added by
patch 0420 and recover the PPPoE/physical hierarchy through the existing
forward-path API. A successful connect creates a persistent session slot
of (session ID, peer MAC). It owns an L3 interface past the per-port ones
and a PPPoE session table entry; disconnect invalidates it. Individual
PPPOE_PUSH flow rules only look up and reference the published session.

The L3 interface carries the source MAC rewrite of the rule together
with the session ID and the PPPoE flag, so the hardware encapsulates
on egress; the session table entry assigns PPPoE ingress of the
session on any physical port to the same interface, which terminates
it. The reverse direction of the connection arrives as a plain rule
of the inner tuple. Netfilter emits no decap key or action, and installs
an ordinary flow entry, which the terminated packets then hit. The
session table write bounds its index against the per-SoC table depth
carried by the variant.

Match the complete vendor session key: enable both unicast and
multicast resolution, validate the peer/server source MAC, and use the
all-physical-port bitmap. The port L3 interface binding is left
untouched by PPPoE flows: session traffic resolves its interface
through the session table, and non-PPPoE ingress on a PPPoE WAN port keeps
taking the software path. A separate lifecycle reference prevents
directional flow expiry from removing a live session.

Program the session dependencies before publishing its valid bits and
invalidate those bits before teardown. Preserve the tunnel-owned EXT2
fields when updating the ordinary L3 mapping. If publication rollback or
disconnect invalidation fails, retain the session key and its dependencies
in a poisoned slot so unknown hardware state cannot be reused.

Also reject rules with match keys outside the supported set instead
of skipping the unknown keys. Tunnel routes, for example, arrive with
ENC_* keys, and offloading them as plain routed flows would have
transmitted the packets unencapsulated. The TCP flags key remains a
deliberate exception: the hardware forwards FIN/RST packets instead
of punting them, which only delays the flow teardown until the flow
idles out.

Use the forward-path context API supplied by the 6.18 backport.
Keep its zero-initialized destination address for PPPoE lookup.

Signed-off-by: Kenneth Kasilag <kenneth@kasilag.me>
Signed-off-by: ViKing <viking.yfy@qq.com>
---
